---
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import { downloads } from '../../config/downloads';
---

<Page
  title="About Niyora"
  description="Neha Prasad on why she built Niyora: what the research on premenstrual mood actually says, what she cut, and what the app will never do with your data."
  ogImage="https://assets.niyora.com/niyora-launch-poster.jpg"
  canonicalUrl="https://niyora.com/about/"
>
  <section class="s left">
    <div class="about">
      <h1>About Niyora</h1>

      <p class="lead">
        Niyora is an app for the days before your period, when your feelings get
        louder than usual. It is built by one person, in Norway, and it is early.
      </p>

      <h2>Who is behind it</h2>
      <img src="/neha.jpg" alt="Neha Prasad" class="photo" />
      <p>
        I'm Neha Prasad. I'm a doctor who wanted to work on women's health. I
        learned design, spent years in health tech as a designer, and now I get
        to build the thing I actually wanted to build.
      </p>

      <h2>Why it exists</h2>
      <p class="pull">
        I used to cry a lot before my period.
      </p>
      <p>
        It got predictable. If I felt low, or didn't want to leave the house, or
        got into a fight with my husband, or felt small at work, I could pretty
        much guess my period was coming. I was reading my own body right. I just
        had nothing to do about it.
      </p>
      <p>
        I saw doctors in three countries. All of them said it's normal and
        there's not much you can do. People told me to try period apps, so I
        did. Every one of them asked me to log how I felt. Anxious. Irritable.
        Moody. I already knew that. I was the one going through it. What I
        wanted was the next sentence, and nobody had one.
      </p>
      <p>So I went and figured it out myself.</p>

      <h2>What I found</h2>
      <p>
        It isn't the hormone drop before your period. Women who feel awful have
        the same hormone levels as women who feel fine. What's different is how
        strongly your brain reacts to that drop. That's personal, so there's no
        one fix. Part of it is lifestyle. The rest is learning to handle
        emotions one at a time.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then I looked at what actually happens in the moment. When you feel low,
        your brain is busy protecting you. It won't rephrase things for you. It
        won't hand you a kinder read. So you react instead of respond. Then you
        deal with what you said, and you regret it.
      </p>
      <p>
        That was me, every month, and no app was touching it. So Niyora predicts
        your week like the others, and then does the part nobody else does: it
        helps you reflect, regulate, and respond in the actual hard hour. Every
        piece of it is one small rep of emotion regulation, done in about a
        minute.
      </p>
      <p class="signature">Neha Prasad. Doctor, builder, founder of Niyora.</p>

      <h2>How I checked it</h2>
      <p>
        I pulled around three thousand papers from Europe PMC and used AI to
        read through them and rewrite what they said in plain English. The 26
        studies Niyora is built on are
        <a class="inline" href="/research/">published and graded</a> strong,
        moderate, or weak, including the weak ones.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then I checked the whole field across
        <a class="inline" href="/research/gaps/">31 questions</a> and published
        that too. There are 168 papers on the genetics of premenstrual symptoms
        and zero real trials. There is thirteen times more research on cycles in
        animals than on premenstrual self-harm. Nobody had put those numbers in
        one place before.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before a fact goes in the app, I have AI agents argue against it. If it
        can't hold up, it doesn't ship. Four claims lost their argument and got
        cut. One of them would have made great copy. Cutting it was right and I
        didn't enjoy it.
      </p>

      <h2>What I gave up</h2>
      <p>
        I wanted the AI to run entirely on your phone, so nothing ever left the
        device. I got it working. It reflected well enough and it gave bad
        suggestions, and the suggestions are the half that has to work. A
        reflection you can't act on is just a nice sentence. So I dropped it and
        kept the privacy promise another way.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also wanted real evidence instead of self-report, by reading heart
        rate variability to find an actual pattern across the cycle. It doesn't
        work. Phones only pick up clean readings when you're still, which is
        almost never during the moments that matter.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both would have been beautiful. Neither would have been good for you.
        Letting them go was most of the work.
      </p>

      <h2>What it will not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>No ads, no profile, no data sold.</li>
        <li>No account needed. Your period dates stay on your device.</li>
        <li>
          Before your words go to Moon's AI, names and identifying details are
          stripped on your phone. What is left can't point back to you.
        </li>
        <li>
          It is not a diagnosis, a therapist, or crisis care. If you're in danger,
          contact your local emergency number.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Because nobody here can read your writing, nobody here can catch a
        mistake either. So the crisis check is built to be jumpy on purpose. A
        plain keyword check runs first, and the AI can never turn it off, only
        on. If it's unsure, it escalates. The long version lives in
        <a class="inline" href="/privacy/">the privacy page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Where it is now</h2>
      <p>
        Niyora is in testing, not on the App Store yet, and there's no revenue.
        That's the order I picked on purpose: the safety and privacy work a
        mental health app has to finish comes before it takes anyone's money. I
        know what it costs to run and I know who it's for. I don't yet know the
        right way to charge for it, and I'd rather say that than invent a
        number.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="inline" href="/changelog/">What's new</a> tracks what ships.
        <a class="inline" href="/talk/">Say hello</a> if you want to shape it.
      </p>

      <div class="links">
        <a class="link" href="/talk/">Say hello</a>
        <a class="link" href={downloads.appStore}>Get the app</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>

<style>
  /* Narrow reading column for the long-form about copy. */
  .about { max-width: 640px; margin: 0 auto; color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.78); }
  .about h2 { margin: 40px 0 14px; }
  .about p, .about li {
    font-size: 15px;
    font-weight: 300;
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }
  .about .pull {
    font-size: 22px;
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.92);
    letter-spacing: -0.2px;
    margin-bottom: 22px;
  }
  .about .signature {
    font-size: 13px;
    letter-spacing: 0.5px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.45);
    margin: 22px 0 28px;
  }
  .about ul { padding-left: 20px; }
  .about li { margin-bottom: 10px; }
  .photo {
    width: 96px;
    height: 96px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    object-fit: cover;
    margin-bottom: 18px;
  }
  a.inline {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.92);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.25);
  }
  a.inline:hover { border-color: rgba(var(--accent-rgb), 0.8); }
  .links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 10px;
    margin-top: 32px;
  }
  .link {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 10px 18px;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.14);
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.92);
    font-size: 13px;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: background 0.18s ease, border-color 0.18s ease, transform 0.18s ease;
  }
  .link:hover {
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.12);
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.36);
    transform: translateY(-1px);
  }
</style>
